\title[Stars, Lines and Curves]{Stars, Lines and Curves: the Berggren Tree of Pythagorean Triples in the Poincar\'e Half-Plane}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{10 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete geometric analysis of the Berggren ternary tree of primitive
Pythagorean triples, embedded in the Poincar\'e upper half-plane $\HH$ by the Euclid map
$z(m,n)=(n+i)/m$. The embedding produces a striking picture --- two pencils
(``stars'') of straight lines radiating from the ideal points $0$ and $1$, a family of
nested arcs, and a single ray marching off at constant speed --- and we prove that every
visible feature is an exact theorem. The organising identity is
$\cosh \dH(i,z(m,n))=(m^{2}+n^{2}+1)/(2m)$, in which the hypotenuse $c=m^{2}+n^{2}$ of
the triple appears as the numerator of a hyperbolic cosine; it forces
$\tfrac12\log c\le \dH\le\tfrac12\log(2(c+1))$ with the lower bound exact. From it we
derive: the identification of the two star pencils by the conserved charges $u=m-n$ and
$n$, and the fact that each star line is a hypercycle at distance $\arsinh u$
(resp.\ $\arsinh n$) from a geodesic; the description of the node set as the coprime odd
grid in charge coordinates, the quantisation of the two stars, and a totient law
$\varphi(2q)$ counting the arms of the line of charge $q$; the boundary dynamics of the
three moves (two parabolic with exact translation normal forms and rate $\Theta(1/k)$
into rational tips, one hyperbolic contracting at rate $4^{-k}$ onto the irrational
silver slope $\sqrt2-1$); a step-length trichotomy, steps tending to $0$ along a star arm
and to $\log(1+\sqrt2)$ along the Pell spine; the reduction of hyperbolic balls to
Euclidean discs, volume growth $\Theta(e^{2R})$, and density of the limit set in $[0,1]$;
and finally an exact collision-distance identity yielding a no-free-lunch theorem: the
ball guaranteed to contain a hypotenuse collision for $N$ already contains $\Theta(N)$
nodes, and the two colliding nodes are pushed apart in proportion to the divisor they
reveal.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, Poincar\'e half-plane, hypercycle,
parabolic and hyperbolic M\"obius dynamics, Pell equation, Euler totient, Euler
factorisation, volume growth}

